WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.985
A typical LLM API architecture includes clients, an API gateway or reverse proxy,

00:00:04.985 --> 00:00:09.871
an application backend, an inference server, a model runtime, model files, a tokenizer,

00:00:09.871 --> 00:00:12.877
compute resources, queues, caches, logs, monitoring, and storage.

00:00:12.877 --> 00:00:16.260
Not every system needs every component on day one,

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:21.145
but each component represents a responsibility that becomes important as the service grows.

00:00:21.145 --> 00:00:24.903
Requests usually begin in application code or a user-facing client.

00:00:24.903 --> 00:00:30.165
The request may pass through a gateway or reverse proxy that handles TLS, routing,

00:00:30.165 --> 00:00:32.419
authentication, rate limits, and policy checks.

00:00:32.419 --> 00:00:36.553
The application backend may add business context, retrieve documents, apply authorization,

00:00:36.553 --> 00:00:38.808
format messages, or select a model.

00:00:38.808 --> 00:00:43.318
The inference server then passes the prepared request to a runtime that

00:00:43.318 --> 00:00:45.573
loads the model and produces output.

00:00:45.573 --> 00:00:50.458
The model runtime depends on model files, tokenizer information, CPU resources, GPU resources,

00:00:50.458 --> 00:00:51.585
memory, and configuration.

00:00:51.585 --> 00:00:53.840
Queues can smooth bursts of demand.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.598
Caches may reduce repeated work for embeddings or common requests.

00:00:57.598 --> 00:01:02.108
Logs and metrics provide visibility into latency, errors, token use, queue depth,

00:01:02.108 --> 00:01:03.235
and resource pressure.

00:01:03.235 --> 00:01:05.866
Storage may hold prompts, outputs, embeddings, configuration,

00:01:05.866 --> 00:01:08.497
or audit records depending on the design.

00:01:08.497 --> 00:01:10.752
Good architecture makes request flow visible.

00:01:10.752 --> 00:01:15.261
Teams should be able to explain how a prompt moves from caller

00:01:15.261 --> 00:01:17.892
to model and how the response returns.

00:01:17.892 --> 00:01:22.026
They should know where authentication happens, where user authorization is checked,

00:01:22.026 --> 00:01:27.287
where sensitive data may be logged, where timeouts apply, and where failures are reported.

00:01:27.287 --> 00:01:29.918
An LLM API is an application service,

00:01:29.918 --> 00:01:33.300
not a single model file with a port open.
